      description: 'Complete guide to obtaining your respiratory therapy license in Texas including TDSHS application, fees, required documents, timeline, and renewal requirements.',
      content: '<h2>Texas Respiratory Therapy Licensure Overview</h2><p>Texas requires all practicing respiratory therapists to hold a valid license issued by the Texas Department of State Health Services (TDSHS). Whether you are a new graduate preparing to enter the workforce or an experienced RT relocating to the Lone Star State, understanding the Texas licensure process is essential. This guide walks you through every step from initial application to ongoing renewal.</p><h3>Governing Board and Authority</h3><p>The Texas Department of State Health Services (TDSHS) oversees respiratory care practitioner licensure under the Texas Respiratory Care Practitioners Act. The Advisory Board of Respiratory Care advises the department on licensing standards, disciplinary actions, and regulatory matters. Texas recognizes two credential levels: Licensed Respiratory Care Practitioner (LRCP) for those holding the CRT credential, and Licensed Respiratory Care Practitioner with an advanced practice designation for those holding the RRT credential.</p><h3>Eligibility Requirements</h3><p>To qualify for a Texas respiratory therapy license, applicants must meet the following criteria:</p><ul><li>Be at least 18 years of age</li><li>Hold a degree from a CoARC-accredited respiratory therapy program</li><li>Hold a valid CRT or RRT credential from the <a href="/divisions/tmc">NBRC</a></li><li>Have no disqualifying criminal history</li><li>Submit a completed application with all required documentation</li></ul><h3>Application Process Step by Step</h3><p>The Texas application process is completed online through the TDSHS licensing portal. Follow these steps to ensure a smooth application:</p><ol><li><strong>Create an online account:</strong> Visit the TDSHS website and register for an online licensing account through the Texas Occupational Licensing portal</li><li><strong>Complete the application form:</strong> Fill out the respiratory care practitioner application, providing personal information, education history, and NBRC credential details</li><li><strong>Submit verification of credentials:</strong> Request that the NBRC send verification of your CRT or RRT credential directly to TDSHS</li><li><strong>Provide educational transcripts:</strong> Have your CoARC-accredited program send official transcripts to the department</li><li><strong>Complete the criminal background check:</strong> Texas requires a fingerprint-based criminal background check through the Texas Department of Public Safety and the FBI</li><li><strong>Pay the application fee:</strong> The initial application fee is approximately $115 to $135, depending on credential level</li><li><strong>Wait for processing:</strong> TDSHS typically processes complete applications within 2 to 4 weeks</li></ol><h3>Temporary Permit</h3><p>Texas offers a temporary permit for new graduates who have completed their respiratory therapy program but have not yet passed the NBRC exam. The temporary permit allows practice under direct supervision while you prepare for your credentialing exams. Temporary permits are valid for a limited period, and you must pass the CRT exam before the permit expires. Use resources like <a href="/signup">NBRCprep</a> to prepare efficiently and pass on your first attempt.</p><h3>Fees Summary</h3><p>Texas licensure involves several fees throughout the process:</p><ul><li><strong>Initial application fee:</strong> Approximately $115 to $135</li><li><strong>Criminal background check:</strong> Approximately $40 to $50</li><li><strong>NBRC credential verification:</strong> Fee set by the NBRC</li><li><strong>Biennial renewal fee:</strong> Approximately $105 to $125</li><li><strong>Late renewal penalty:</strong> Additional fees apply if renewal is submitted after the expiration date</li></ul><h3>License Renewal Requirements</h3><p>Texas respiratory therapy licenses must be renewed every two years. Renewal requires completion of continuing education (CE) hours. Texas requires 24 CE hours per renewal cycle, which must include specific topics such as ethics, jurisprudence, and clinical practice updates. At least 12 of these hours must be directly related to respiratory care practice. Keep thorough records of your CE activities, as TDSHS may audit licensees for compliance.</p><h3>Continuing Education Details</h3><p>Acceptable CE providers include the AARC, state respiratory therapy societies, CoARC-accredited programs, and other approved organizations. Online courses, conferences, and in-service programs may all count toward your CE requirements. Texas also accepts NBRC specialty credential maintenance activities as CE credit.</p><h3>Out-of-State Applicants</h3><p>If you hold a respiratory therapy license in another state, Texas requires a full application rather than a simple endorsement process. You must still provide NBRC credential verification, educational transcripts, and complete a criminal background check. However, having an active license in another state may expedite the review process. Contact TDSHS directly for the most current requirements regarding out-of-state transfers.</p><h3>Tips for a Smooth Application</h3><p>Start your application process early, ideally 4 to 6 weeks before you plan to begin working. Ensure your NBRC credentials are current and request verification promptly. Complete your fingerprinting as soon as possible, as background check processing can add time. Double-check your application for accuracy before submitting to avoid delays. If you have not yet passed your NBRC exams, begin studying now with <a href="/divisions/tmc">TMC practice exams</a> and <a href="/divisions/nps">clinical simulation prep</a>.</p><p><a href="/signup">Start practicing free</a> with NBRCprep\'s flashcards and timed exams, or <a href="/pricing">view study plans</a> for full access to all 6 NBRC divisions.</p>',
