      description: 'Step-by-step guide to California respiratory therapy licensure through the Respiratory Care Board, including jurisprudence exam, fees, and renewal process.',
      content: '<h2>California Respiratory Therapy Licensure Overview</h2><p>California maintains some of the most rigorous respiratory therapy licensing standards in the country. The Respiratory Care Board of California (RCB) governs all respiratory care practitioners in the state, requiring not only national NBRC credentials but also a state-specific jurisprudence examination. This comprehensive guide covers everything you need to know about obtaining and maintaining your California RT license.</p><h3>The Respiratory Care Board of California</h3><p>The RCB operates under the California Department of Consumer Affairs and is dedicated exclusively to regulating respiratory care practitioners. Unlike states where respiratory therapy falls under a broader medical board, California\'s dedicated board focuses specifically on RT standards, enforcement, and public protection. The board sets licensure requirements, investigates complaints, and enforces disciplinary actions.</p><h3>Eligibility Requirements</h3><p>California requires the following for respiratory therapy licensure:</p><ul><li>Completion of a CoARC-accredited respiratory therapy program (associate degree or higher)</li><li>Current CRT or RRT credential from the <a href="/divisions/tmc">NBRC</a></li><li>Passing score on the California Respiratory Care Board law and ethics examination (jurisprudence exam)</li><li>No disqualifying criminal convictions</li><li>Social Security number (required by California law for professional licensing)</li></ul><h3>The California Jurisprudence Exam</h3><p>One of the unique requirements for California licensure is the state jurisprudence examination. This test covers California respiratory care laws and regulations, ethical standards, and scope of practice. The exam is administered through PSI testing centers and covers topics including:</p><ul><li>The Respiratory Care Practice Act and its regulations</li><li>Scope of practice limitations and authorized procedures</li><li>Patient rights and informed consent</li><li>Mandatory reporting requirements</li><li>Professional conduct and disciplinary processes</li><li>Supervision requirements</li></ul><p>The jurisprudence exam is computer-based and typically consists of multiple-choice questions. Study the California Business and Professions Code and the California Code of Regulations related to respiratory care before attempting the exam.</p><h3>Application Process</h3><ol><li><strong>Submit the application online:</strong> Create an account on the RCB BreEZe online licensing system and complete the application for respiratory care practitioner license</li><li><strong>Pay the application fee:</strong> The application and initial license fee is approximately $225 to $275</li><li><strong>Request credential verification:</strong> Have the NBRC send verification of your CRT or RRT credential directly to the RCB</li><li><strong>Submit educational verification:</strong> Your CoARC-accredited program must verify your completion directly to the board</li><li><strong>Complete the background check:</strong> California requires Live Scan fingerprinting at an authorized location, with results sent to both the California Department of Justice and the FBI</li><li><strong>Pass the jurisprudence exam:</strong> Schedule and pass the California law and ethics exam through PSI</li><li><strong>Await license issuance:</strong> Processing typically takes 4 to 8 weeks for complete applications</li></ol><h3>Fees Breakdown</h3><ul><li><strong>Application and initial license fee:</strong> Approximately $225 to $275</li><li><strong>Live Scan fingerprinting:</strong> Approximately $50 to $80 (varies by location)</li><li><strong>Jurisprudence exam fee:</strong> Approximately $50 to $75</li><li><strong>Biennial renewal fee:</strong> Approximately $225 to $275</li><li><strong>Delinquent renewal penalty:</strong> Additional fees for late renewal</li></ul><h3>License Renewal</h3><p>California RT licenses expire every two years. Renewal requires 15 hours of approved continuing education per renewal cycle. At least one hour must cover California law and ethics. The RCB accepts CE from AARC-approved providers, accredited educational institutions, and other approved organizations. Renew through the BreEZe system before your expiration date to avoid penalties and practice interruptions.</p><h3>Important California-Specific Rules</h3><p>California has several notable regulations that differ from other states. The state does not allow respiratory therapists to practice under a temporary or provisional license while waiting for full licensure, though there is a limited supervised practice exception during the application period. California also requires RTs to maintain malpractice awareness and report any malpractice settlements or judgments to the board. Practicing without a valid license in California can result in criminal charges.</p><h3>Out-of-State Transfers</h3><p>California does not have reciprocity agreements with other states. Out-of-state RTs must complete the full application process, including the jurisprudence exam. If you are relocating to California, begin the application process well in advance of your move. Prepare for the jurisprudence exam by studying California-specific respiratory care laws and consider preparing for your NBRC credential renewal with <a href="/signup">NBRCprep study tools</a>.</p><p><a href="/signup">Start practicing free</a> with NBRCprep\'s flashcards and timed exams, or <a href="/pricing">view study plans</a> for full access to all 6 NBRC divisions.</p>',
