      description: 'Complete guide to Florida respiratory therapy licensure through the Department of Health, including application steps, CE requirements, fees, and renewal.',
      content: '<h2>Florida Respiratory Therapy Licensure Overview</h2><p>Florida is one of the largest employers of respiratory therapists in the United States, and the state requires all practicing RTs to hold a valid license issued by the Florida Department of Health (DOH). The Board of Respiratory Care, operating under the DOH, manages all aspects of RT licensure in the Sunshine State. This guide provides a detailed walkthrough of the licensure process from start to finish.</p><h3>Governing Authority</h3><p>The Florida Board of Respiratory Care operates under Chapter 468, Part V of the Florida Statutes. The board consists of respiratory therapists and public members appointed by the governor. Florida issues a single respiratory therapist license, though the state recognizes both CRT and RRT credentials from the <a href="/divisions/tmc">NBRC</a> for different scope-of-practice considerations.</p><h3>Eligibility Requirements</h3><p>To qualify for a Florida respiratory therapy license, you must:</p><ul><li>Be at least 18 years of age</li><li>Complete a respiratory therapy education program approved by CoARC or an equivalent accrediting body</li><li>Hold a valid CRT or RRT credential from the NBRC</li><li>Be of good moral character with no disqualifying criminal history</li><li>Meet the requirements for the background screening under Florida law</li></ul><h3>Application Process</h3><ol><li><strong>Apply online through MQA Services:</strong> The Florida Department of Health uses the MQA (Medical Quality Assurance) online portal for all professional license applications</li><li><strong>Complete the application form:</strong> Provide personal information, education details, employment history, and credential information</li><li><strong>Pay the application fee:</strong> Initial application and licensing fees total approximately $200 to $250</li><li><strong>Request NBRC credential verification:</strong> Have the NBRC send direct verification to the Florida Board of Respiratory Care</li><li><strong>Submit educational verification:</strong> Request that your respiratory therapy program provide official verification of program completion</li><li><strong>Complete background screening:</strong> Florida requires electronic fingerprinting through an approved vendor, with results submitted to both the Florida Department of Law Enforcement (FDLE) and the FBI</li><li><strong>Wait for review and approval:</strong> Complete applications are typically processed within 2 to 4 weeks</li></ol><h3>Fees Summary</h3><ul><li><strong>Application fee:</strong> Approximately $100</li><li><strong>Initial licensing fee:</strong> Approximately $100 to $150</li><li><strong>Fingerprinting fee:</strong> Approximately $45 to $55</li><li><strong>Biennial renewal fee:</strong> Approximately $100 to $130</li><li><strong>Late renewal fee:</strong> Additional penalty applies after expiration</li></ul><h3>Continuing Education Requirements</h3><p>Florida requires 24 hours of continuing education per biennial renewal cycle. The CE requirements include specific mandatory topics:</p><ul><li><strong>Medical errors prevention:</strong> 2 hours covering patient safety and error prevention strategies</li><li><strong>Laws and rules:</strong> 2 hours on Florida respiratory care laws and regulations</li><li><strong>HIV/AIDS:</strong> 1 hour on HIV/AIDS education (required for initial licensure and every third renewal cycle)</li><li><strong>Domestic violence:</strong> 1 hour (required for initial licensure)</li><li><strong>General respiratory care CE:</strong> Remaining hours in clinical or professional topics</li></ul><p>Approved CE providers include the AARC, Florida Society of Respiratory Care, and other board-approved organizations.</p><h3>License Renewal</h3><p>Florida RT licenses expire on a biennial cycle. The DOH sends renewal notices approximately 90 days before expiration. Renew online through the MQA portal. If your license expires, you may still renew within a grace period by paying the renewal fee plus a late fee. However, practicing on an expired license is a violation of Florida law and may result in disciplinary action. Set calendar reminders well in advance of your renewal date.</p><h3>Temporary License</h3><p>Florida offers a temporary license for new graduates who have completed an approved respiratory therapy program and applied for NBRC examination. The temporary license allows supervised practice while you prepare for and complete your credentialing exams. Temporary licenses are valid for a limited period, typically around 6 months. Use this time wisely and prepare with <a href="/divisions/nps">practice exams</a> to ensure you pass before the temporary license expires.</p><h3>Out-of-State Applicants</h3><p>Florida offers licensure by endorsement for respiratory therapists licensed in other states. The endorsement process requires a valid, unrestricted license in another state, NBRC credential verification, a background check, and payment of applicable fees. Endorsement applications may be processed more quickly than initial applications. Florida does not require an additional state exam beyond the NBRC credentials.</p><h3>Tips for Success</h3><p>Apply for your Florida license as early as possible, especially if relocating for employment. The fingerprinting process can sometimes create delays, so complete it promptly. Keep copies of all submitted documents for your records. If you are a new graduate, start preparing for your NBRC exams immediately with <a href="/signup">NBRCprep</a> to make the most of your temporary license period.</p><p><a href="/signup">Start practicing free</a> with NBRCprep\'s flashcards and timed exams, or <a href="/pricing">view study plans</a> for full access to all 6 NBRC divisions.</p>',
