      description: 'Guide to New York respiratory therapy licensure through NYSED, including limited permit process, application fees, and continuing education requirements.',
      content: '<h2>New York Respiratory Therapy Licensure Overview</h2><p>New York State requires respiratory therapists to hold a license issued by the New York State Education Department (NYSED). The Board of Respiratory Therapy, operating under NYSED\'s Office of the Professions, establishes licensing standards and oversees the profession throughout the state. New York has unique requirements that differ from many other states, making it important to understand the process thoroughly before applying.</p><h3>Governing Authority</h3><p>The NYSED Office of the Professions manages all health professional licensing in New York, including respiratory therapy. Unlike most states where licensure falls under a department of health, New York places respiratory therapy under its education department. This structure means the application process follows NYSED\'s specific procedures, which can differ from what you may have experienced in other states.</p><h3>Eligibility Requirements</h3><p>New York has specific educational and examination requirements for RT licensure:</p><ul><li>Completion of a respiratory therapy program registered with NYSED or an equivalent program approved by the department</li><li>Passing the NBRC <a href="/divisions/tmc">TMC examination</a> (CRT or RRT credential)</li><li>Be of good moral character</li><li>Be at least 18 years of age</li><li>Meet specific coursework requirements if your program was completed outside New York</li></ul><h3>The Limited Permit</h3><p>One of New York\'s distinctive features is the limited permit system. New graduates who have completed an approved respiratory therapy program but have not yet passed the NBRC exam can apply for a limited permit. This permit allows supervised practice under specific conditions:</p><ul><li>The limited permit is valid for one year from the date of issuance</li><li>Practice must occur under the supervision of a licensed respiratory therapist or physician</li><li>The permit is not renewable, so you must obtain full licensure within the permit period</li><li>You must demonstrate that you have applied for or scheduled your NBRC examination</li></ul><p>Use the limited permit period wisely. Focus your study efforts with <a href="/signup">NBRCprep</a> to ensure you pass the NBRC exam and qualify for full licensure before the permit expires.</p><h3>Application Process</h3><ol><li><strong>Complete the NYSED application:</strong> Submit Form 1, the Application for Licensure, available through the NYSED Office of the Professions website</li><li><strong>Pay the application fee:</strong> The application fee is approximately $175 to $200, payable to the New York State Education Department</li><li><strong>Submit educational verification:</strong> Your respiratory therapy program must complete Form 2, the Certification of Professional Education, and submit it directly to NYSED</li><li><strong>Provide NBRC credential verification:</strong> Have the NBRC send verification of your exam results directly to NYSED</li><li><strong>Request score transfer:</strong> If applicable, ensure your NBRC scores are sent through the appropriate channels</li><li><strong>Submit additional documentation:</strong> Out-of-state or international graduates may need additional evaluations or coursework verification</li><li><strong>Wait for processing:</strong> NYSED processing times can range from 4 to 12 weeks depending on application volume</li></ol><h3>Fees</h3><ul><li><strong>Application fee:</strong> Approximately $175 to $200</li><li><strong>Limited permit fee:</strong> Approximately $35 to $70</li><li><strong>Triennial registration fee:</strong> Approximately $40 to $65</li><li><strong>Late registration fee:</strong> Additional penalties for expired registrations</li></ul><h3>Registration vs. Licensure</h3><p>New York distinguishes between licensure and registration. Your license is permanent once granted, but you must maintain active registration to practice. Registration must be renewed every three years. Practicing on an expired registration is a violation of New York Education Law and can result in disciplinary action, even though your underlying license remains valid.</p><h3>Continuing Education Requirements</h3><p>New York does not currently mandate specific continuing education hours for respiratory therapist registration renewal, which is unique among the larger states. However, the NBRC requires CE for credential maintenance, and employers may have their own CE requirements. Staying current with clinical advances and best practices is essential regardless of state requirements. Consider using <a href="/divisions/tmc">TMC</a> and <a href="/divisions/nps">clinical simulation</a> practice tools to reinforce your knowledge.</p><h3>Out-of-State and International Applicants</h3><p>Respiratory therapists licensed in other states must complete the full NYSED application process. New York does not offer licensure by endorsement in the traditional sense. International graduates must have their education evaluated for equivalency by NYSED, which may require additional coursework. Processing times for out-of-state and international applications tend to be longer, so plan accordingly.</p><h3>Practice Tips</h3><p>Start your New York application well before you plan to begin working. NYSED processing times are often longer than other states due to the volume of professional license applications they handle. Ensure all forms are sent from the issuing institution directly to NYSED, as the department does not accept applicant-submitted copies of educational verification or credential reports.</p><p><a href="/signup">Start practicing free</a> with NBRCprep\'s flashcards and timed exams, or <a href="/pricing">view study plans</a> for full access to all 6 NBRC divisions.</p>',
