      description: 'Complete guide to Ohio respiratory therapy licensure through the Ohio Medical Board, including application steps, fees, timeline, and renewal requirements.',
      content: '<h2>Ohio Respiratory Therapy Licensure Overview</h2><p>Ohio requires respiratory therapists to hold a valid license issued by the State Medical Board of Ohio (MRB). The board regulates respiratory care professionals under the Ohio Revised Code and maintains standards for both initial licensure and ongoing practice. Ohio\'s process is straightforward compared to some states, but understanding each step ensures you can begin practicing without unnecessary delays.</p><h3>Governing Authority</h3><p>The State Medical Board of Ohio oversees respiratory therapy licensure along with other allied health professions. The board\'s Respiratory Care Section processes applications, manages renewals, and handles disciplinary matters. Ohio recognizes respiratory care professionals at the Registered Respiratory Therapist (RRT) level, and applicants must hold the NBRC <a href="/divisions/tmc">RRT credential</a> for full licensure.</p><h3>Eligibility Requirements</h3><ul><li>Graduation from a CoARC-accredited respiratory therapy program</li><li>Current RRT credential from the NBRC</li><li>Completion of a criminal background check</li><li>Good moral character as determined by the board</li><li>Be at least 18 years of age</li></ul><h3>Application Process</h3><ol><li><strong>Complete the online application:</strong> Visit the eLicense Ohio portal and create an account to access the respiratory care license application</li><li><strong>Submit the application form:</strong> Fill out all required fields including personal information, education history, and credential details</li><li><strong>Pay the application fee:</strong> The initial application fee is approximately $100 to $150</li><li><strong>Request NBRC verification:</strong> Have the NBRC send direct verification of your RRT credential to the Ohio Medical Board</li><li><strong>Provide educational documentation:</strong> Your CoARC-accredited program must verify your completion directly to the board</li><li><strong>Complete the criminal background check:</strong> Ohio requires fingerprint-based background checks through the Bureau of Criminal Investigation (BCI) and the FBI</li><li><strong>Submit any additional documentation:</strong> The board may request references, employment verification, or other supporting materials</li><li><strong>Await board review:</strong> Complete applications are typically processed within 2 to 6 weeks</li></ol><h3>Fees</h3><ul><li><strong>Application fee:</strong> Approximately $100 to $150</li><li><strong>Background check fee:</strong> Approximately $40 to $60</li><li><strong>Biennial renewal fee:</strong> Approximately $100 to $125</li><li><strong>Late renewal penalty:</strong> Additional fees apply for renewals submitted after expiration</li></ul><h3>Limited Permit</h3><p>Ohio offers a limited permit for graduates of approved respiratory therapy programs who have not yet obtained the RRT credential. The limited permit allows practice under the direct supervision of a licensed respiratory therapist or physician. Permits are time-limited, and holders must actively pursue their NBRC credentials. Prepare for your exams with <a href="/signup">NBRCprep</a> to pass on your first attempt and transition to full licensure as quickly as possible.</p><h3>License Renewal</h3><p>Ohio respiratory therapy licenses must be renewed biennially. The renewal process requires completion of continuing education and payment of the renewal fee. Ohio requires 12 hours of continuing education per renewal cycle. CE activities must be relevant to respiratory care practice and obtained from approved providers.</p><h3>Continuing Education Details</h3><p>Acceptable CE activities for Ohio renewal include:</p><ul><li>AARC-approved continuing education programs</li><li>Courses offered by CoARC-accredited educational programs</li><li>Hospital-based in-service education programs</li><li>Professional conferences and seminars</li><li>Online CE courses from approved providers</li></ul><p>Keep documentation of all CE activities for at least two years beyond the renewal cycle, as the board may audit licensees for compliance.</p><h3>Out-of-State Applicants</h3><p>Ohio provides a pathway for respiratory therapists licensed in other states to obtain Ohio licensure. You must still complete the full application, provide NBRC credential verification, and pass the background check. Having an active, unrestricted license in another state can support your application, but Ohio requires all applicants to meet the same credential and educational standards.</p><h3>Ohio-Specific Considerations</h3><p>Ohio\'s scope of practice for respiratory therapists is defined in the Ohio Revised Code and includes traditional respiratory care procedures, arterial blood gas sampling, bronchoscopy assistance, and hemodynamic monitoring. Ohio law requires RTs to practice under the direction of a physician, though the level of supervision varies by practice setting. Familiarize yourself with Ohio\'s scope of practice to understand your legal boundaries.</p><h3>Getting Started</h3><p>Begin your Ohio licensure process as soon as you graduate from your respiratory therapy program. The earlier you apply, the sooner you can begin working. If you are still preparing for the NBRC exam, take advantage of <a href="/divisions/nps">clinical simulation practice</a> and <a href="/divisions/tmc">TMC exam review</a> materials to build confidence and pass your credentialing exams efficiently.</p><p><a href="/signup">Start practicing free</a> with NBRCprep\'s flashcards and timed exams, or <a href="/pricing">view study plans</a> for full access to all 6 NBRC divisions.</p>',
