      description: 'Step-by-step guide to Pennsylvania respiratory therapy licensure through the PA Department of State, including application process, fees, and renewal.',
      content: '<h2>Pennsylvania Respiratory Therapy Licensure Overview</h2><p>Pennsylvania requires all respiratory therapists to hold a license issued by the Pennsylvania Department of State (DOS), Bureau of Professional and Occupational Affairs. The State Board of Medicine oversees respiratory therapist licensing in the Commonwealth. Pennsylvania\'s process includes specific requirements that applicants should understand before beginning the application.</p><h3>Governing Authority</h3><p>In Pennsylvania, respiratory therapist licensure falls under the State Board of Medicine within the Department of State. This differs from some states that have a dedicated respiratory care board. The board establishes educational, examination, and character requirements for licensure and handles disciplinary matters. Pennsylvania issues licenses to both Certified Respiratory Therapists (CRT) and Registered Respiratory Therapists (RRT) based on the NBRC credentials held.</p><h3>Eligibility Requirements</h3><ul><li>Graduation from a respiratory therapy program accredited by CoARC</li><li>Current CRT or RRT credential from the <a href="/divisions/tmc">NBRC</a></li><li>Completion of a criminal background check</li><li>Good moral character</li><li>Payment of all applicable fees</li></ul><h3>Application Process</h3><ol><li><strong>Access the PALS system:</strong> Pennsylvania uses the Pennsylvania Licensing System (PALS) for all professional license applications. Create an account at the PALS website</li><li><strong>Complete the application:</strong> Select the respiratory therapist license application and fill out all required sections</li><li><strong>Pay the application fee:</strong> The initial application fee is approximately $45 to $75</li><li><strong>Submit NBRC credential verification:</strong> Request that the NBRC send credential verification directly to the State Board of Medicine</li><li><strong>Provide educational verification:</strong> Your CoARC-accredited program must submit proof of graduation to the board</li><li><strong>Complete the criminal background check:</strong> Pennsylvania requires a state criminal history record check through the Pennsylvania State Police, plus an FBI background check for applicants who have not been Pennsylvania residents for the past two consecutive years</li><li><strong>Wait for processing:</strong> Standard processing time is approximately 4 to 8 weeks for complete applications</li></ol><h3>Fees</h3><ul><li><strong>Application fee:</strong> Approximately $45 to $75</li><li><strong>State criminal background check:</strong> Approximately $22</li><li><strong>FBI background check (if required):</strong> Approximately $24 to $28</li><li><strong>Biennial renewal fee:</strong> Approximately $45 to $65</li><li><strong>Late renewal surcharge:</strong> Additional fee for renewals after expiration</li></ul><h3>Temporary Practice Permit</h3><p>Pennsylvania offers a temporary practice permit for new graduates and out-of-state applicants who have submitted a complete application for licensure. The temporary permit allows supervised practice while the application is being processed. Temporary permits are valid for a specific period and are not renewable. If you are a new graduate awaiting your NBRC exam results, use this time to continue studying with <a href="/signup">NBRCprep</a> resources.</p><h3>License Renewal</h3><p>Pennsylvania respiratory therapy licenses expire biennially. The renewal deadline falls on an even-year cycle, and the board sends renewal notifications before the expiration date. Continuing education is required for renewal.</p><h3>Continuing Education Requirements</h3><p>Pennsylvania requires 20 hours of continuing education per biennial renewal cycle. The CE requirements include:</p><ul><li>All hours must be in subjects related to respiratory care practice</li><li>Activities must be offered by AARC-approved providers, accredited educational institutions, or other board-approved organizations</li><li>At least a portion of CE must include clinical respiratory care topics</li><li>Online and in-person activities both qualify</li></ul><p>Maintain records of your CE activities including certificates of completion, dates, sponsoring organizations, and topics covered. The board may audit your CE compliance at any time during or after the renewal cycle.</p><h3>Out-of-State Applicants</h3><p>Pennsylvania offers licensure by endorsement for respiratory therapists licensed in other states with substantially similar requirements. The endorsement pathway requires proof of current licensure in another state, NBRC credential verification, and a background check. Endorsement applications may be processed more quickly than initial applications, but all documentation must be complete for efficient processing.</p><h3>Scope of Practice</h3><p>Pennsylvania\'s Respiratory Therapist Practice Act defines the scope of practice for licensed RTs. The scope includes diagnostic testing, therapeutic procedures, and patient assessment activities performed under the direction of a physician. Pennsylvania\'s scope of practice is consistent with national standards and includes arterial blood gas analysis, ventilator management, airway management, and pulmonary rehabilitation services.</p><h3>Key Reminders</h3><p>Start the application process early, especially if you need an FBI background check. The PALS system can be navigated efficiently if you have all documents ready before beginning. Keep copies of all submitted materials. If preparing for your NBRC exams, take advantage of <a href="/divisions/tmc">TMC practice tests</a> and <a href="/divisions/nps">clinical simulations</a> to build your exam readiness.</p><p><a href="/signup">Start practicing free</a> with NBRCprep\'s flashcards and timed exams, or <a href="/pricing">view study plans</a> for full access to all 6 NBRC divisions.</p>',
