      description: 'Complete guide to Illinois respiratory therapy licensure through IDFPR, including application steps, required documents, fees, and renewal process.',
      content: '<h2>Illinois Respiratory Therapy Licensure Overview</h2><p>Illinois requires all respiratory care practitioners to hold a license issued by the Illinois Department of Financial and Professional Regulation (IDFPR). The state licenses respiratory therapists under the Respiratory Care Practice Act, which establishes standards for education, credentialing, and professional conduct. This guide covers every step of the Illinois licensure process, from initial application through ongoing renewal.</p><h3>Governing Authority</h3><p>IDFPR is the umbrella agency that manages professional licensing in Illinois across dozens of professions, including respiratory care. The Respiratory Care Section within IDFPR processes applications, manages renewals, and oversees compliance with state regulations. The Respiratory Care Advisory Board advises the department on matters related to respiratory care practice standards and regulations.</p><h3>Eligibility Requirements</h3><ul><li>Completion of a respiratory therapy program accredited by CoARC</li><li>Current CRT or RRT credential from the <a href="/divisions/tmc">NBRC</a></li><li>No disqualifying criminal history</li><li>Be at least 18 years of age</li><li>United States Social Security number</li></ul><h3>Application Process</h3><ol><li><strong>Apply online through IDFPR:</strong> Visit the IDFPR website and navigate to the respiratory care practitioner section to access the online application</li><li><strong>Complete the application form:</strong> Provide personal details, education history, and NBRC credential information</li><li><strong>Pay the application fee:</strong> The initial application and license fee is approximately $80 to $125</li><li><strong>Request NBRC credential verification:</strong> Have the NBRC send verification of your CRT or RRT credential directly to IDFPR</li><li><strong>Submit proof of education:</strong> Your CoARC-accredited program must provide direct verification of your graduation to the department</li><li><strong>Complete fingerprinting:</strong> Illinois requires fingerprint-based criminal background checks through an approved vendor</li><li><strong>Await processing:</strong> IDFPR typically processes complete applications within 4 to 8 weeks</li></ol><h3>Fees</h3><ul><li><strong>Application fee:</strong> Approximately $50 to $75</li><li><strong>Initial license fee:</strong> Approximately $30 to $50</li><li><strong>Fingerprinting fee:</strong> Approximately $50 to $65</li><li><strong>Biennial renewal fee:</strong> Approximately $50 to $75</li><li><strong>Restored license fee (lapsed renewal):</strong> Higher fees apply for restoring an expired license</li></ul><h3>Temporary License</h3><p>Illinois issues temporary licenses to new graduates who have completed a CoARC-accredited program and are awaiting their NBRC exam results. The temporary license allows supervised practice for a limited period. You must pass your NBRC exam and obtain full licensure before the temporary license expires. Make the most of this period by preparing with <a href="/signup">NBRCprep practice exams</a> and <a href="/divisions/tmc">TMC review materials</a>.</p><h3>License Renewal</h3><p>Illinois respiratory therapy licenses expire biennially. Renewal requires completion of continuing education and payment of the renewal fee. The renewal process is completed through IDFPR\'s online system.</p><h3>Continuing Education Requirements</h3><p>Illinois requires 24 hours of continuing education per biennial renewal cycle. Specific requirements include:</p><ul><li>CE hours must be directly related to respiratory care practice</li><li>Activities must be offered by AARC-approved providers, accredited educational institutions, or IDFPR-approved organizations</li><li>A portion of CE must address professional ethics or respiratory care law</li><li>Online courses, workshops, conferences, and academic coursework all qualify</li></ul><p>Document all CE activities thoroughly. IDFPR conducts random audits and may require you to provide certificates of completion, attendance records, and descriptions of CE activities.</p><h3>Out-of-State Applicants</h3><p>Illinois offers licensure by endorsement for respiratory therapists holding valid licenses in other states. Endorsement requires proof of an active, unrestricted license, NBRC credential verification, and completion of the Illinois background check. The endorsement pathway can streamline the application process, but all required documentation must be submitted before a license is issued.</p><h3>Illinois-Specific Scope of Practice</h3><p>The Illinois Respiratory Care Practice Act defines the scope of practice broadly, including diagnostic and therapeutic procedures, patient assessment, and ventilator management. Illinois requires respiratory therapists to work under the direction of a licensed physician but allows significant professional autonomy in clinical decision-making within the established scope. Respiratory therapists in Illinois may perform arterial puncture, capillary blood sampling, and other invasive procedures as defined in the act.</p><h3>Tips for a Smooth Process</h3><p>The IDFPR online system can sometimes experience delays during peak application periods. Submit your application as early as possible and monitor your application status through the online portal. If you encounter delays, contact IDFPR\'s licensure division directly. Keep copies of all submitted documents and confirmation numbers for your records. If you have not yet passed your NBRC exams, prioritize your study plan with <a href="/divisions/nps">clinical simulation practice</a> and comprehensive review resources.</p><p><a href="/signup">Start practicing free</a> with NBRCprep\'s flashcards and timed exams, or <a href="/pricing">view study plans</a> for full access to all 6 NBRC divisions.</p>',
