      content: '<h2>Georgia Respiratory Therapy Licensure Overview</h2><p>Georgia requires respiratory therapists to hold a license issued by the Georgia Composite Medical Board (GCMB). The board regulates respiratory care practitioners under the Georgia Respiratory Care Act and maintains standards for education, examination, and professional conduct. Georgia\'s licensing process is manageable when you understand each step and prepare your documentation in advance.</p><h3>Governing Authority</h3><p>The Georgia Composite Medical Board handles respiratory therapy licensure as part of its broader mandate to regulate medical professionals in the state. The board\'s respiratory care section manages applications, renewals, and disciplinary proceedings. Georgia licenses respiratory care professionals at two levels based on NBRC credentials, recognizing both the CRT and RRT designations from the <a href="/divisions/tmc">NBRC</a>.</p><h3>Eligibility Requirements</h3><ul><li>Graduation from a CoARC-accredited respiratory therapy program</li><li>Current CRT or RRT credential from the NBRC</li><li>No disqualifying criminal background</li><li>Good moral character</li><li>Be at least 18 years of age</li></ul><h3>Application Process</h3><ol><li><strong>Download or access the application:</strong> Visit the Georgia Composite Medical Board website to obtain the respiratory care practitioner license application</li><li><strong>Complete the application form:</strong> Fill out all sections including personal information, education details, and employment history</li><li><strong>Pay the application fee:</strong> The initial application fee is approximately $100 to $150</li><li><strong>Submit NBRC credential verification:</strong> Request that the NBRC send direct verification of your credentials to the GCMB</li><li><strong>Provide official transcripts:</strong> Have your respiratory therapy program send official transcripts directly to the board</li><li><strong>Complete the criminal background check:</strong> Georgia requires a fingerprint-based background check through the Georgia Crime Information Center and the FBI</li><li><strong>Provide passport-style photographs:</strong> Some application cycles require recent photographs with your application</li><li><strong>Wait for board review:</strong> The GCMB typically processes complete applications within 4 to 8 weeks, though processing times may vary based on board meeting schedules</li></ol><h3>Fees</h3><ul><li><strong>Application fee:</strong> Approximately $100 to $150</li><li><strong>Background check fee:</strong> Approximately $40 to $55</li><li><strong>Biennial renewal fee:</strong> Approximately $75 to $125</li><li><strong>Late renewal fee:</strong> Additional penalty for expired licenses</li><li><strong>Reinstatement fee:</strong> Higher fees apply if your license has been lapsed for an extended period</li></ul><h3>Temporary Permit</h3><p>Georgia issues temporary permits to graduates of accredited respiratory therapy programs who have applied for licensure but have not yet completed all requirements. The temporary permit allows supervised practice while you complete the NBRC examination process. Temporary permits have a defined expiration and are not renewable. Use this time to prepare intensively for your credentialing exams with <a href="/signup">NBRCprep study tools</a>.</p><h3>License Renewal</h3><p>Georgia respiratory therapy licenses must be renewed every two years. The renewal process requires completion of continuing education and payment of the renewal fee. Renewal can be completed online through the GCMB\'s licensing portal.</p><h3>Continuing Education Requirements</h3><p>Georgia requires 20 hours of continuing education per biennial renewal cycle. Requirements include:</p><ul><li>All CE hours must be relevant to respiratory care practice</li><li>Activities must come from AARC-approved providers, CoARC-accredited programs, or other board-recognized organizations</li><li>A variety of formats are accepted including live seminars, online courses, and professional conferences</li><li>Retain CE documentation for at least four years after completion</li></ul><h3>Out-of-State Applicants</h3><p>Georgia offers a pathway for respiratory therapists licensed in other states to obtain Georgia licensure. Applicants must submit a complete application, provide verification of licensure from their current state, demonstrate current NBRC credentials, and pass the Georgia background check. Having an active license in good standing in another state can support your application, but you must still meet all Georgia-specific requirements.</p><h3>Georgia-Specific Considerations</h3><p>Georgia\'s scope of practice for respiratory therapists includes standard respiratory care procedures, patient assessment, and treatment planning under physician direction. The state has experienced growing demand for respiratory therapists, particularly in the Atlanta metropolitan area and other urban centers. Georgia offers competitive compensation and a lower cost of living compared to many other states with large RT populations.</p><h3>Getting Started</h3><p>Begin your Georgia licensure application promptly after completing your respiratory therapy program or deciding to relocate to the state. Gather all required documentation before starting the application to avoid processing delays. If you are preparing for your NBRC exams, take advantage of comprehensive review resources including <a href="/divisions/tmc">TMC practice exams</a> and <a href="/divisions/nps">clinical simulation preparation</a>.</p><p><a href="/signup">Start practicing free</a> with NBRCprep\'s flashcards and timed exams, or <a href="/pricing">view study plans</a> for full access to all 6 NBRC divisions.</p>',
